  "clues": {
    "kinetics": "Protein binding is variable, falling from 51% to 7% across the concentration range. Urinary excretion over 24 hours is about 11% to 14% of an intravenous dose.",
    "substance": "Supplied as the dihydrate, a white crystalline powder; the active moiety has a molecular weight of 749 and the dihydrate 785.",
    "product": "Approved under an NDA, supplied as 500 mg single-dose vials and given once daily.",
    "safety": "No boxed warning. Contraindicated with known hypersensitivity to related antibacterials, or a history of cholestatic jaundice or hepatic dysfunction from it; diarrhea and nausea are most common.",
    "indication": "Community-acquired pneumonia in adults and pelvic inflammatory disease in adults, when caused by susceptible bacteria.",
    "mechanism": "Binds the 23S rRNA of the 50S ribosomal subunit of susceptible bacteria, inhibiting protein synthesis and impeding assembly of that subunit."
  },
  "tell": "Its 68-hour terminal half-life, prolonged by release from tissues, sets this macrolide apart from its rivals.",
